The \( \text{Ksp} \) values for the sulfides are:
- \( \text{MnS} \): \( 1.4 \times 10^{-10} \)
- \( \text{NiS} \): \( 1.6 \times 10^{-10} \)
- \( \text{CuS} \): \( 1.0 \times 10^{-10} \)
- \( \text{HgS} \): \( 1.0 \times 10^{-10} \)

The \( \text{Ksp} \) of \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) is \( 1.0 \times 10^{-10} \). 

For precipitation to occur, the product of \( [\text{S}^{2-}] \) and \( [\text{M}^{2+}] \) must exceed the \( \text{Ksp} \) of the sulfide. 

The \( \text{Ksp} \) of \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) is \( \text{Ksp} = [\text{H}_2\text{S}]^2 [\text{S}^{2-}] \). 

Since the solution is acidified, the \( [\text{H}^+] \) is high, and the \( [\text{S}^{2-}] \) is very low. 

Thus, the sulfides that will precipitate are:
- \( \text{CuS} \): \( \text{Ksp} = 1.0 \times 10^{-10} \)
- \( \text{HgS} \): \( \text{Ksp} = 1.0 \times 10^{-10} \)
- \( \text{MnS} \): \( \text{Ksp} = 1.4 \times 10^{-10} \)
- \( \text{NiS} \): \( \text{Ksp} = 1.6 \times 10^{-10} \)

All the sulfides will precipitate because their \( \text{Ksp} \) values are close to the \( \text{Ksp} \) of \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \). 

