An investigator analyzes a small gene sequenced during the Human Genome Project and observes that one strand of the DNA molecule contains 20 adenines (A), 25 guanines (G), 30 cytosines (C), and 22 thymines (T). How many of each base (A, G, C, T) are present in the complete double-stranded DNA molecule?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","In a double-stranded DNA molecule, adenine (A) always pairs with thymine (T), and guanine (G) always pairs with cytosine (C). 

The given sequence is from one strand, so the other strand must have the complementary base counts. 

- Adenine (A) on the first strand = 20, so Thymine (T) on the other strand = 20.
- Guanine (G) on the first strand = 25, so Cytosine (C) on the other strand = 25.
- Cytidine (C) on the first strand = 30, so Guanine (G) on the other strand = 30.
- Thymine (T) on the first strand = 22, so Adenine (A) on the other strand = 22.

Now, we add the counts from both strands:

- Adenine (A) = 20 (strand 1) + 22 (strand 2) = 42
- Guanine (G) = 25 (strand 1) + 30 (strand 2) = 55
- Cytinine (C) = 30 (strand 1) + 25 (strand 2) = 55
- Thymine (T) = 22 (strand 1) + 20 (strand 2) = 42

So, the complete double-stranded DNA molecule contains:
- 42 adenine (A) molecules
- 55 guanine (G) molecules
