","The reaction between solid benzoic acid (H(C₇H₅O₂)) and sodium hydroxide (NaOH) is an acid-base reaction, specifically a neutralization reaction.

1. **Observable Effects:**
   - When solid benzoic acid is added to a solution of sodium hydroxide, the solid dissolves in the solution.
   - The reaction is exothermic, meaning it releases heat. This can be observed as an increase in the temperature of the solution.
   - If the solution is acidic before the reaction, the pH will increase as the acid is neutralized. If the solution is basic, the pH will decrease.
   - If the reaction is carried out in a solution with an indicator, the color of the indicator may change to indicate the pH change.

2. **Fully Ionized Equation:**
   The reaction between benzoic acid (H(C₇H₅O₂)) and sodium hydroxide (NaOH) can be represented as follows:

   \[
   \text{H(C₇H₅O₂)(s)} + \text{NaOH(aq)} \rightarrow \text{H}_2\text{O(l)} + \text{NaHCO₃(aq)}
   \]

   This equation shows that solid benzoic acid reacts with sodium hydroxide in aqueous solution to produce water and sodium bicarbonate (NaHCO₃).",fluency,"# Task Description
