A solution containing 0.5126 g of naphthalene (molecular weight = 128.17 g/mol) in 50 g of carbon tetrachloride (CCl₄) exhibits a boiling point elevation of 0.402°C. Another solution containing 0.6216 g of an unknown solute in the same mass of CCl₄ shows a boiling point elevation of 0.647°C. Calculate the molecular weight of the unknown solute.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To find the molecular weight of the unknown solute, we use the boiling point elevation formula:

\[
\Delta T = K_b \cdot m
\]

where:
- \(\Delta T\) is the boiling point elevation
- \(K_b\) is the ebullioscopic constant of the solvent
- \(m\) is the molality of the solution

**Step 1: Calculate \(K_b\) for CCl₄**
For naphthalene, we have:
\[
\Delta T = 0.402 \, \text{°C}
\]
\[
0.5126 \, \text{g} = \frac{0.5126}{128.17} \, \text{mol} = 0.0040 \, \text{mol}
\]
\[
m = \frac{0.0040 \, \text{mol}}{0.050 \, \text{kg}} = 0.08 \, \text{mol/kg}
\]
\[
0.402 = K_b \times 0.08
\]
\[
K_b = \frac{0.402}{0.08} = 5.03 \, \text{°C/mol/kg}
\]

**Step 2: Calculate the molality of the unknown solute**
For the unknown solute, we have:
\[
\Delta T = 0.647 \, \text{°C}
\]
\[
0.6216 \, \text{g} = \frac{0.6216}{M} \, \text{mol}, \quad \text{where } M = 5.03 \, \text{°C/mol/kg}
\]
\[
m = \frac{0.6216 / M}{0.050 \, \text{kg}} = \frac{0.6216}{0.050 \times M} \, \text{mol/kg}
\]
\[
