"What is the decay length of the evanescent wave in a GaAs core and Ga\(_{0.2}\)Al\(_{0.8}\)As cladding, where \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \sim 0.3\), for a wavelength of 1 μm?","<bos><start_of_turn>user
What is the decay length of the evanescent wave in a GaAs core and Ga\(_{0.2}\)Al\(_{0.8}\)As cladding, where \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \sim 0.3\), for a wavelength of 1 μm?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The decay length (\(\lambda_d\)) of an evanescent wave is given by:
\[
\lambda_d = \frac{\lambda}{2\pi \Delta n}
\]
where:
- \(\lambda\) is the wavelength of the light,
- \(\Delta n\) is the refractive difference between the material of the waveguide and the surrounding medium.

Here, the evanescent wave is evanescent in the cladding material (Ga\(_{0.2}\)Al\(_{0.8}\)As), and the refractive difference is given as \(\Delta n = n_{\text{eff}} - n_f = 0.3\), where \(n_{\text{eff}}\) is the refractive index of the cladding material and \(n_f\) is the refractive index of the free medium (e.g., air or air-like medium).

The wavelength is given as \(\lambda = 1 \mu m = 1 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{m}\).

Substituting these values into the formula:
\[
\lambda_d = \frac{1 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{m}}{2\pi \times 0.3} = \frac{1 \times 10^{-6}}{1.885} \, \text{m} \approx 5.3 \times 10^{-7} \, \text{m} = 530 \, \text{nm}
\]

Thus, the decay length of the evanescent wave is \(\boxed{530 \, \text{nm}}\).",relevance,"# Task Description
