The decay length (\(\lambda_d\)) of an evanescent wave is given by:
\[
\lambda_d = \frac{\lambda}{2\pi \Delta n}
\]
where:
- \(\lambda\) is the wavelength of the light,
- \(\Delta n\) is the refractive difference between the material of the waveguide and the surrounding medium.

Here, the evanescent wave is evanescent in the cladding material (Ga\(_{0.2}\)Al\(_{0.8}\)As), and the refractive difference is given as \(\Delta n = n_{\text{eff}} - n_f = 0.3\), where \(n_{\text{eff}}\) is the refractive index of the cladding material and \(n_f\) is the refractive index of the free medium (e.g., air or air-like medium).

