","To determine the increase in total entropy, we follow these steps:

1. **Heat transferred to the gases:**
   The heat removed from the gases is:
   \[
   Q_{\text{gas}} = m_{\text{gas}} \times C_p \times \Delta T = m_{\text{gas}} \times 1.005 \times (1100 - 550) = 552.75 \times m_{\text{gas}} \text{ kJ}
   \]

2. **Heat transferred to the water:**
   The heat required to vaporize 1 kg of water at 220°C is:
   \[
   Q_{\text{water}} = 1858.5 \text{ kJ/kg} \times 1 \text{ kg} = 1858.5 \text{ kJ}
   \]

3. **Heat balance:**
   Since all heat transferred from the gases goes to the water:
   \[
   Q_{\text{gas}} = Q_{\text{water}} \implies 552.75 \times m_{\text{gas}} = 1858.5 \implies m_{\text{gas}} = \frac{1858.5}{552.75} \approx 3.36 \text{ kg}
   \]

4. **Entropy change of the gases:**
   The entropy change of the gases is:
   \[
   \Delta S_{\text{gas}} = m_{\text{gas}} \times C_p \ln \left( \frac{1100}{550} \right) = 3.36 \times 1.005 \ln \left( \frac{1100}{550} \right) \approx 3.36 \times 1.005 \times 1.44 = 4.86 \text{ J/K}
   \]

