","The net chemical reaction for the discharging of a lead-sulfur dioxide battery is:  
\[ \text{Pb} + \text{PbSO}_4 + \text{H}_2\text{SO}_4 \rightarrow 2\text{PbSO}_4 + \text{H}_2\text{O} \]  
This reaction occurs at the anode (left side) and the cathode (right side) of the battery. The overall reaction is the sum of the half-reactions:  
- **Anode (oxidation):**  
\[ \text{Pb} + \text{H}_2\text{SO}_4 \rightarrow \text{PbSO}_4 + 2\text{H}^+ + \text{e}^- \]  
- **Cathode (reduction):**  
\[ \text{PbSO}_4 + 2\text{H}^+ + \text{e}^- \rightarrow \text{PbSO}_4 + \text{H}_2\text{O} \]  

Thus, the overall reaction is the sum of these two half-reactions.  

